<!--
  THE CALENDAR IS PART OF THE PRODUCT — Field Note 01, ~20 minutes.
  Source: essays/07-the-calendar-is-part-of-the-product.md
  SEMANTIC ACCENT (chosen in advance, one only): EARLY — a puzzle served before
  its local day has arrived. Monotone Red marks that state and nothing else
  (the leaked card, its arrow and its label). Everything else is black, white
  and greys.
-->
<Deck title="The Calendar Is Part of the Product" brand="THE CALENDAR" series="FIELD NOTE 01 — THE BOUNDARY">
<deck-cue time="COVER · 00:00–00:45" title="The title">Open on the story, not the lesson. A player in Los Angeles was handed London’s tomorrow before Los Angeles reached midnight. Everything after this is how one small sentence — “one puzzle per local day” — broke across five systems.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 1 · 00:45–02:00" title="Each part was reasonable">Walk the stack top to bottom. The server stores a puzzle per calendar date. The generator builds tomorrow ahead of time. The browser only learns its timezone after the first render. The player was promised one puzzle per local day. Nobody wrote a bad line. The bug lives between the boxes.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 2 · 02:00–04:00" title="One instant, two dates">Slow down here, this is the whole talk. Watch the cursor: 00:30 UTC. London already rolled over an hour and a half ago, so it is May 21. Los Angeles is at half past five in the afternoon, still May 20. An instant is global. A calendar date is local. This is not an edge case, it happens every single day.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 3 · 04:00–05:30" title="Today decides four things">“Today” is not a label on the screen. It picks which puzzle you see, which date owns your attempt, which guesses count, and what ops calls missing or late. If those use different clocks, you see one puzzle and save progress against another. Streaks vanish. Solved boards reset.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 4 · 05:30–07:00" title="UTC was a default">The first helper defaulted to UTC. Deterministic, which feels safe, and wrong for a local game. The fix is small: take a timezone argument. But the real move is naming the two days. Canonical day for generation and inventory, UTC on purpose. Player day for everything the player touches, and the zone is passed in.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 5 · 07:00–09:00" title="The first request is provisional">The server cannot run Intl for your browser. So the first response is allowed to be wrong, with a safe fallback. After mount the browser reads its zone, stores a cookie, and if the date differs it revalidates. The first request may be provisional. The browser may not stay provisional once it knows better.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 6 · 09:00–10:30" title="A cookie is input">The cookie is client-supplied: missing, malformed, or forged. Decode it, ask Intl whether it is a real IANA zone, and fall back to UTC if not. No IP geolocation. VPNs and mobile networks make it worse, and the browser already knows the exact zone it is using.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 7 · 10:30–13:00" title="The leak">The production bug. RealiTea pre-generates future puzzles on purpose. The resolver tried today’s exact date, found nothing ready, and fell back to the most recently created row. At 00:30 UTC that row was May 21. Los Angeles asked for May 20 and got ERIKA, tomorrow’s answer. Red is the only colour in the deck, and it only ever means early.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 8 · 13:00–14:30" title="Eligible, not newest">The query answered “which puzzle was created most recently?” The product needed “which eligible puzzle, on or before this local date, was created most recently?” One bound. Date keys sort as strings, so less-or-equal is correct; keep that visible so nobody replaces it with timestamp math later.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 9 · 14:30–16:00" title="Decide once">Loading the puzzle and loading the attempt must not compute “today” separately. One resolver takes the instant and the zone and returns the puzzle that was actually served. The attempt is looked up against that served date, including in the grace period when yesterday is the fallback.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 10 · 16:00–17:30" title="A day is not 24 hours">Once you have a date key, adding twenty-four hours to a local timestamp is wrong twice a year. Spring forward is a 23 hour day, fall back is 25. So: instant to date uses the zone. Date to adjacent date uses calendar arithmetic, parsed at UTC midnight as a stable space.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 11 · 17:30–18:45" title="The test that told the truth">Tests must fix the instant, name the zone, and have data on both sides of the boundary. A test using the current time passes or fails depending on where CI runs. Also assert what was passed to the repository, so a refactor cannot sneak the unbounded query back behind a lucky mock.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 12 · 18:45–19:30" title="Six rules">Read them fast, the shape matters more than the wording: date keys as identity, zone makes the date, cookie is input, UTC on purpose, bound every fallback, resolve once.</deck-cue>
<deck-cue time="STEP 13 · 19:30–20:00" title="The question">“Most recent” was not enough. “Today” was not enough. The system became correct when both words got a boundary. Leave them with the question to ask in every scheduler: whose today is it?</deck-cue>

